# %% [markdown]
# ## 16. Self-check
#
# 1. Two inputs have exactly the same shortfall for a declined applicant, and the notice has one place
#    left. What makes the notice defensible?
#    - (a) either is fine, since they are tied, and both are principal reasons to the same degree
#    - (b) whichever the sort routine put first, since that ranking came from the code that scored them
#    - (c) a rule fixed in advance, such as the lower input number first, so the notice can be regenerated
#
# 2. For an additive scorecard, with the development rows as the background, an input's exact Shapley
#    value for one applicant equals:
#    - (a) its own points for that applicant minus its mean points over the background
#    - (b) its coefficient times the input's standard deviation over the development rows
#    - (c) its points divided by the applicant's total points, whatever the background is
#
# 3. A background of a few hundred development rows, standing for "all applicants", gives different
#    first reasons for some applicants than the whole population would, by a different amount for
#    each equally valid sample. Suppose a different reference population changes the first reason of
#    more applicants than that spread does. A fair reading is:
#    - (a) the choice of reference does not matter, since any sample gives the same attributions
#    - (b) the change is real to the extent it exceeds the spread of the sampling error, which falls with size
#    - (c) a tree model's attributions cannot be used for reasons, since a tree has no coefficients
#
# 4. A reason is flagged by the screen: it is cited far more often for the group under test, its input
#    separates the two groups, and it is cited often. The right next step is:
#    - (a) conclude the model is unlawful, since a flag on all three counts is itself the proof
#    - (b) conclude nothing, since the model never sees the group and the flag must be chance
#    - (c) review it: ask what the input adds, what a model without it would cost, and take legal advice
#
# 5. Why add up the shortfalls of the inputs that share a reason code before choosing reasons?
#    - (a) a cause measured by two columns splits its points, so a two-place notice can name it twice
#    - (b) to shorten the notice, so that the reasons fit on one line and meet the rule that it be short
#    - (c) because the regulation requires one reason code per input, cited once in the order of the score
#
# Answers, with the reasoning, come with this lesson's worked solution when you enrol on Synapsa.

# %% [markdown]
# One last cell, and it is the deliverable. A set of reasons does not hand on as a table: it hands on
# with the rule that chose them, the reference it was measured against, the tests it passed and failed,
# and the things that were never checked, or someone will later quote it as though it described a real
# lender's notices.

# %%
def _reason_note() -> None:
    if not _ready("reason note", "decision", "challenger", "boost", "audit", "card_reasons", "sampling", "books"):
        return
    dec, ch, verdicts = S["decision"], S["challenger"], S["audit"]
    floor_lo, floor_med, floor_hi = _spread(S["sampling"][N_BG]["first, vs exact"])
    books = {kind: [r[kind]["ref_flip"] for r in S["books"]] for kind in ("scorecard", "challenger")}
    print("REASON-CODE NOTE — hand this on with the models")
    print(f"  rule         the principal reasons are the (at most) {NOTICE_REASONS} inputs with the largest strictly positive shortfall; ties go to the")
    print("               lower input number; fewer than that if fewer than that cost the applicant anything")
    print(f"  scorecard    weight-of-evidence classes pooled to each input's documented direction, whole points ({PDO} points double the odds),")
    print(f"               cut-off {dec.cut} (the lowest {DECLINE_SHARE:.0%} of development applicants are declined); shortfall against the reference's average points, in whole points")
    print(f"  challenger   {len(ch['model'].booster.trees)} monotone-constrained trees; exact interventional Shapley attributions on the points scale against "
          "the whole reference population")
    print(f"  references   'all applicants' and 'at or just above the cut-off' (cut-off up to cut-off + {MARGINAL_WIDTH} points), each made of every development row that "
          "qualifies; the reasons")
    print(f"               differ in order for {S['card_ref_flip']['ordered']:.1%} (scorecard) and {S['boost']['ref_flip']['ordered']:.1%} (challenger) of declined applicants "
          f"between them (book 0);")
    print(f"               the first reason changes for {min(books['scorecard']):.1%} to {max(books['scorecard']):.1%} (scorecard) and {min(books['challenger']):.1%} to "
          f"{max(books['challenger']):.1%} (challenger) over {len(S['books'])} books; a {N_BG}-row sample of 'all applicants'")
    print(f"               would change the challenger's first reason for a median {floor_med:.1%} (smallest {floor_lo:.1%}, largest {floor_hi:.1%} over "
          f"{N_SAMPLES} samples)")
    print(f"  stability    a move of {EPS_MAIN:g} development standard deviations, up and down, on the continuous inputs; tolerance {STABILITY_TOL:.0%} of the reason set")
